{-
   A tuple written as a match pivot is never built.

   `match {lhs, rhs}` pairs two scrutinees so that one match can look at both, and the tuple exists
   only to be taken apart by the very next thing that reads it. Building it anyway was a double
   free: a tuple is contiguous storage, so its fields are *copied* in, and a copy of a value with a
   teardown is a second owner of it. `Eq(Maybe(a))`'s `==` written this way answered four drops for
   two objects - it consumed both operands it was only lent.

   Nothing in matching was at fault. resolvePattern only sinks the pivot when the pattern *writes* a
   convention, so `Just(x)` binds where the value lies and the pivot goes on owning it; the copy was
   upstream, in resolving `{lhs, rhs}` as an ordinary tuple expression before it was ever a pivot.
   So the fix is not to match through a borrow, it is not to build the tuple: the elements are
   already values, and matching against them directly is the same tests with no storage in between.

   The counter is what asserts it. A pivot that copies and a pivot that does not are identical in
   the answers the match produces - the difference is only in how many times a teardown runs.
-}

let &drops = 0 :: Int

data Held {n: Int}

instance Drop(Held):
    fn drop(->self: Held) -> {}:
        drops = drops + 1

instance Eq(Held):
    fn ==(lhs: Held, rhs: Held) -> Bool = lhs.n == rhs.n
    fn !=(lhs: Held, rhs: Held) -> Bool = !(lhs == rhs)

-- Two borrowed operands, paired. Neither may be consumed.
fn sameMaybe(lhs: Maybe(Held), rhs: Maybe(Held)) -> Bool = match {lhs, rhs}:
    {Just(x), Just(y)} -> x == y
    {Nothing, Nothing} -> True
    _ -> False

-- Three of them, and a nested pattern inside one position, so the decomposition is not only the
-- one-level case.
fn ordered(a: Maybe(Held), b: Maybe(Held), c: Int) -> Int = match {a, b, c}:
    {Just(x), Just(y), 0} -> x.n + y.n
    {Just(x), Nothing, n} -> x.n + n
    _ -> 0

{-
   The two fallbacks, both in one function: the pivot names its fields, and an alternative binds the
   pivot whole rather than taking it apart. Either one means the tuple has to exist - there is
   nothing for `whole` to be bound to otherwise, and a named field pattern refers to field names an
   unnamed literal does not have - so this match builds it exactly as it always did.

   Written over `Int` on purpose. A built tuple of *borrowed* values with a teardown is what the
   silent-handover check now rejects, which is the right answer for a pivot that is genuinely stored
   and the reason the decomposition above is worth having.
-}
fn labelled(x: Int, y: Int) -> Int = match {first: x, second: y}:
    {first: 0, second: 0} -> 100
    whole -> whole.first + whole.second

fn main() -> Int:
    let &total = 0 :: Int

    if True:
        let a = Just(Held {n: 3})
        let b = Just(Held {n: 4})

        -- False: 3 and 4 differ. What matters is that asking did not consume either operand.
        if sameMaybe(a, b) then total = total + 1000

        total = total + ordered(a, b, 0)

    {-
       Both died at the end of the block above, once each.

       Asserted as a total rather than as a zero after each call, because the drop belongs at the
       *last use* of a value and each call above is one - a pivot that copied its operands would
       have run their teardown inside the match too, and that is the difference this counts. Two is
       right; four was the bug.
    -}
    total = total + drops * 10

    total = total + labelled(2, 5)

    -- 7 from `ordered`, 20 from the two drops, and 7 from `labelled`'s second alternative: 34.
    return total
